\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Gyroscope rotation over the true image interval.} \textbf  {a}, The released inertial windows hold 11 samples per image pair, read as ten steps of 10 ms, but the image interval is about 104 ms, so every frame pair loses about 4\% of its rotation (blue). \textbf  {b}, Processing of one frame pair. The lag and the IMU-to-camera rotation $\mathbf  {R}_{ci}$ are fitted on training drives only: the lag on a grid from $-80$ to $+80$ ms (steps of 10 ms, then 2.5 ms) minimising the median residual after a rigid (Kabsch) alignment.}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:timing}{{1}{5}{\tbf {Gyroscope rotation over the true image interval.} \tbf {a}, The released inertial windows hold 11 samples per image pair, read as ten steps of 10 ms, but the image interval is about 104 ms, so every frame pair loses about 4\% of its rotation (blue). \tbf {b}, Processing of one frame pair. The lag and the IMU-to-camera rotation $\mathbf {R}_{ci}$ are fitted on training drives only: the lag on a grid from $-80$ to $+80$ ms (steps of 10 ms, then 2.5 ms) minimising the median residual after a rigid (Kabsch) alignment}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Architecture.} The camera and the inertial sensor feed a frozen learned network (shaded; only its direction of translation $\mathbf  {d}_\mathrm  {net}$ and step length $\ell _\mathrm  {net}$ are used) and three measurements that involve no learning. Two-view direction uses the gyroscope rotation, and accelerometer length uses the gyroscope attitudes; each measurement comes with a quality signal. The observability gate (blue outline) combines them for every frame pair and component. The dashed online-trust block belongs to the post-hoc version 4 only and estimates how far the network can be trusted from its agreement with confident geometry. Details of the network and of the gate decisions are in Supplementary Figs.~S1--S3.}}{6}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pipeline}{{2}{6}{\tbf {Architecture.} The camera and the inertial sensor feed a frozen learned network (shaded; only its direction of translation $\mathbf {d}_\mathrm {net}$ and step length $\ell _\mathrm {net}$ are used) and three measurements that involve no learning. Two-view direction uses the gyroscope rotation, and accelerometer length uses the gyroscope attitudes; each measurement comes with a quality signal. The observability gate (blue outline) combines them for every frame pair and component. The dashed online-trust block belongs to the post-hoc version 4 only and estimates how far the network can be trusted from its agreement with confident geometry. Details of the network and of the gate decisions are in Supplementary Figs.~S1--S3}{figure.2}{}}
